#import "@preview/mitex:0.2.5": mi, mitex

= ECON 0100 | Vignette B3 | Equilibrium

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  = Solutions

]

_Answers and work are shown in the red blocks._

Members of the wizarding world have preferences for pumpkin pasties according to the following demand curve:

#mitex(`
P_d = 14 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d
`)

Pumpkin pasties are produced by many sellers according to the following supply curve:

#mitex(`
P = 2 + \frac{1}{2} Q_s
`)

Prices are in galleons and quantity is in pasties.

== Q1 | Equilibrium

Find and plot the equilibrium price and quantity for pumpkin pasties.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  $Q^* = 12$ pasties at $P^* = 8$ galleons.

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      *Plot.* Demand: vertical intercept $14$, horizontal intercept $0 = 14 - \frac{1}{2} Q_d \rightarrow Q_d = 28$. Supply: vertical intercept $2$, rising by $1$ galleon for every $2$ pasties.

      *Equilibrium.* At equilibrium $Q_s = Q_d = Q^*$, so set the two curves equal:

      #mitex(`
      2 + \tfrac{1}{2} Q^* = 14 - \tfrac{1}{2} Q^* \rightarrow Q^* = 12
      `)

      Plug $Q^*$ back into either curve:

      #mitex(`
      P^* = 2 + \tfrac{1}{2} \cdot 12 = 2 + 6 = 8
      `)

      Star the pair $(12, 8)$ where the curves cross.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

== Q2 | Surplus

Find and plot the consumer and producer surplus in the market equilibrium you found in Q1.

#block(width: 100%, stroke: (left: 2pt + rgb("#c00000")), inset: (left: 1em))[
  #set text(fill: rgb("#c00000"))

  CS $= 36$ galleons. PS $= 36$ galleons.

  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, 1fr),
    gutter: 1em,
    [
      Consumer surplus is the triangle below demand and above $P^*$, out to $Q^*$:

      #mitex(`
      \text{CS} = h \cdot b \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = (14 - 8) \cdot 12 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 36
      `)

      Producer surplus is the triangle above supply and below $P^*$, out to $Q^*$:

      #mitex(`
      \text{PS} = h \cdot b \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = (8 - 2) \cdot 12 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 36
      `)

      Total surplus is $36 + 36 = 72$ galleons.
    ],
    [
      #image("data:image/svg+xml;base64,", width: 100%)
    ],
  )
]

